A||||9||jmw||URGENT!! Doors Won't Close||||11||
Z||000000||jmw||09-15-2000||03:17 PM||||This is the damndest thing. I was examining a sticker on the the driver's side door and accidentally pushed in the mechanism that allows the door to close. Apparently this thing moves upward, in two stages, and in the process of trying to figure out how it works, I pushed it up another notch.  It is now in a locked position and will not budge downward at all. Believe me, I have tried everything.<P>To make matters worse, I then looked at the passenger side door and did the same f'ing thing, except that one only went up one position before it locked.<P>In short, neither one of my doors will close and I am up s*** creek. Can someone please tell me how to remedy this situation ASAP? I feel absolutely ridiculous, but it really is serious because I obviously cannot drive like this and if it rains I am screwed.<P>Thank you. ||||reg||11
Z||000001||yobtah||09-15-2000||03:50 PM||||I did this once too... I don't completely remember how I fixed it, but it wasn't too bad.  Basically, find a screwdriver or similar tool to act as the "pin" that the door would usually latch onto.  It's the one on the rear door sill that's about two inches long and half an inch in diameter.  If you slide the scredriver into the notch in the door where that pin would go, you should be able to operate the lock and latch on the door as if the door were really closed.  I don't remember enough for better instructions... if you look at the latch carefully and simulate the pin's purpose with the screwdriver, you should be able to "unlock" and "open" the door as if it were really closed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||09-15-2000||04:20 PM||||What the fu........ are you doing? (J/K) Make sure your door is unlocked to begin with.  As you say, the latch, in the door closed as if the door was shut.  I'm not sure if you tried this but, stick your index finger in where the striker in the door jam would actually be.  Lift your door handle as if you're going to open the door.  Apply pressure with your finger acting as a striker pin.(towards the car as if pulling the door away from the car.  the latch should rotate down back into the position it would be in as if you opened the door.  I hope this helps.  It's nice when the problem is a simple one.  Or take a 10 pound sledge and go for it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Kidding again I think?||||reg||3
Z||000003||JohnnyK||09-15-2000||04:21 PM||||If it's the same problem I have in my (I can't really tell by the description), hold the door handle before prying.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||09-15-2000||04:24 PM||||Just unlock the doors!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||10
Z||000005||whamel||09-15-2000||04:31 PM||||My trans am used to do this all the time when it got the slightest bit cold out.  (Poor lubrication in the latch.)  Here's the simplified procedure I used to use for that POS:<BR>Door unlocked<BR>Handle up<BR>Pry door latch with metal object, I caught my finger once, not doing that again!<BR>SNAP, SNAP (pretty loud if I remember right when you do it manually)<BR>     Hope this works for you - sometimes it helps to have two people as it's tough to hold the handle all the way up and manipulate the latch.||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||09-15-2000||04:52 PM||||Not a big deal, First make sure the door is not locked, pull up on the outside or inside door handle, and use a screwdriver or rod to rotate the latch.  I've did a similar thing with a glove box, gives you that "Ohhh $%*!" what do I do feeling.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">     <BR>     <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000007||stsmithgt||09-15-2000||05:01 PM||||I hear an echo on this thread  thread thread thread   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I said to use your finger,  It's never too late to learn, you might pinch your finger in your latch. 8)What's safety first? <P>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 09-15-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||jmw||09-15-2000||05:02 PM||||God love you, one and all. <P>Don't know why this didn't occur to me. Makes perfect sense. Believe combination of panic and frustration obliterated any chance for logical thought process to occur. Honestly, sometimes the littlest things can turn into the biggest crises... <P>Thank you SO MUCH for your help.||||reg||10
Z||000009||Monkeyman||09-15-2000||08:13 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jmw:<BR><B>Don't know why this didn't occur to me. Makes perfect sense. Believe combination of panic and frustration obliterated any chance for logical thought process to occur. Honestly, sometimes the littlest things can turn into the biggest crises...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Been there, done that (on far too many occasions).  Next time, take a break, drink an ice cold (insert beverage of your choice), and cool off for a while.  The frustration will decrease and the thought process will return.  I have to consciously do this or my blood pressure would kill me (if my wife didn't 1st).<P><p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
